<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  polkit actions for idlepolicyd.

  Installed at /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/. Local overrides belong in
  /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/, which the package does not touch.

  The defaults below follow one principle, applied consistently: an action that can
  only ever make the machine STAY AWAKE is cheap to get wrong and is allowed freely;
  an action that can make it SLEEP is expensive to get wrong and is gated. A machine
  that stays awake wrongly costs electricity. One that sleeps wrongly loses work.

  Note especially the lease action: allow_inactive is `yes`, which is unusual. It is
  correct here precisely because a lease cannot cause a sleep, and because the caller
  who needs one most, somebody over ssh starting a six-hour job, is by definition
  in a session logind considers inactive. Requiring authentication there would mean
  the one caller with no way to answer a prompt is the one being prompted.
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>idlectl</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/Eric-Canas/cachyos-idlectl</vendor_url>

  <action id="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest">
    <description>Let this machine rest now</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to let this machine rest now.</message>
    <!--
      Satisfies exactly two blocks, fixed by condition name: `always` and
      `after_resume`, i.e. the base schedule and the post-resume settle window. Every
      other block is still evaluated, so this cannot interrupt a game, a download, a
      held lease or an open remote session, and neither can it clear the vetoes that
      are not blocks (a detector that cannot answer, a configuration file that had to
      be rejected). No configuration key can widen what this action reaches.

      Allowed for anyone at the machine: it is no more than saying "I am done", and
      the person in front of the screen can already press the power button.
      Authentication is required remotely, because a remote caller cannot see whether
      somebody else is sitting at it.
    -->
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>yes</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest-forced">
    <description>Force this machine to rest, overriding every safeguard</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to force this machine to rest, overriding a running game, an active download, a held lease and the presence of anyone using it.</message>
    <!--
      The only way to suspend a machine somebody is actively typing on.

      It installs a ceiling due immediately, so it defeats every floor there is: every
      `never`, human presence, the veto raised by a detector that cannot answer and
      the veto raised by a rejected configuration file. That last pair is what it is
      usually reached for: a machine wedged awake by something broken rather than by
      something happening. That is also why it cannot be folded into
      `io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest`: this is a strictly larger power, and a
      credential that may ask a machine to rest must not thereby be able to force one
      that somebody is using.

      auth_admin_keep even for a caller sitting at the machine, and no `yes` anywhere:
      this overrides the human-presence floor, so the fact that a human is present is
      not evidence that this particular action is wanted. `_keep` so that a legitimate
      operator running several of these in a row is not worn down into automating the
      prompt away.
    -->
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.lease">
    <description>Hold this machine awake while a job runs</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to hold this machine awake.</message>
    <!--
      A lease can only prevent sleep, never cause it, and it is bounded twice over: it
      dies with the file descriptor that holds it, and it expires at its TTL. The
      worst outcome of granting it too freely is a machine that stayed awake and can
      be inspected with `idlectl lease list`.

      allow_inactive=yes is the deliberate part. The canonical caller is a remote
      session starting long work, and logind considers such a session inactive; there
      is nobody there to answer a prompt. Denying it would push people towards
      disabling the whole daemon, which is a strictly worse outcome.
    -->
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>yes</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>yes</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.reload">
    <description>Reload the idle policy configuration</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to reload the idle policy configuration.</message>
    <!--
      Reloading applies whatever is currently in /etc/idlectl, which is
      administrator-owned; being able to trigger it is close enough to being able to
      change policy that it is gated the same way. A file that cannot be parsed is
      dropped as a whole and named in the journal, the layers that still parse take
      effect, and the three sleep actions are held until the fault is fixed, so a
      reload can leave a machine awake, but never without a policy.
    -->
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
